{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Most array solutions are built from a few small, reusable pieces. You'll write them so often that they should become automatic — like knowing your times tables. Here are the ones that matter most."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Sum, minimum and maximum"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "One pass, one or two variables. The only subtle part is the **starting value**: start `max` at the first element (or at `-Infinity`), never at 0 — an array of negative numbers would otherwise report 0 as its maximum."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function stats(nums) {\n  let sum = 0, min = nums[0], max = nums[0];\n  for (const x of nums) {\n    sum += x;\n    if (x < min) min = x;\n    if (x > max) max = x;\n  }\n  return { sum, min, max };\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def stats(nums):\n    total, low, high = 0, nums[0], nums[0]\n    for x in nums:\n        total += x\n        low = min(low, x)\n        high = max(high, x)\n    return total, low, high"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "static long[] stats(int[] nums) {\n    long sum = 0;\n    int min = nums[0], max = nums[0];\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        sum += x;\n        if (x < min) min = x;\n        if (x > max) max = x;\n    }\n    return new long[] { sum, min, max };\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "tuple<long long, int, int> stats(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    long long sum = 0;\n    int mn = nums[0], mx = nums[0];\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        sum += x;\n        mn = min(mn, x);\n        mx = max(mx, x);\n    }\n    return {sum, mn, mx};\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In Java and C++ the sum is a `long`/`long long`: adding up many large `int`s can overflow 32 bits (about 2.1 billion) without any warning."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Step through a maximum search yourself — this is the simplest dry run in the course:"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "find-max"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Linear search"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "\"Where is `target`?\" in an unsorted array: check every slot and stop at the first match. Return `-1` to mean \"not found\" — a value that can never be a real index."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function indexOf(nums, target) {\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    if (nums[i] === target) return i;\n  }\n  return -1;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def index_of(nums, target):\n    for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n        if x == target:\n            return i\n    return -1"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "static int indexOf(int[] nums, int target) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n        if (nums[i] == target) return i;\n    }\n    return -1;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int indexOf(const vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); i++) {\n        if (nums[i] == target) return i;\n    }\n    return -1;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "That's **O(n)**. If the array is **sorted**, binary search does it in **O(log n)** — you'll use that idea in several problems later."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Swapping two elements"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Swapping needs a temporary variable: if you write `a = b` first, the old value of `a` is gone before you can copy it."
    },
